<p>What went out is the network as it runs, not a demo cut for the occasion and not a subset with the difficult parts sanded off. The registry, the TEG (the gateway every movement of value passes through) and the ledger that keep every frame's books. The auditor, a separate service that reads those books once a minute and belongs to no registry. The console, Pocket and the SDKs. The testers, which use all of it every night the way a person would, and the way a person who is trying to break it would. The templates every frame and every cloud operator is born from, the kit's tools, and the documentation, which was read against the code before it was allowed out. The server is licensed AGPL-3.0-or-later and the SDKs Apache-2.0.</p>

<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p52-explore.jpg" alt="Discovery on the EU console, seen without an account. A header reads six registries, one network. Below it the frames and operators as chips, EU, ASIA, Amsterdam, Lisbon, Ho Chi Minh City and Shenzhen, all active and synced, the federated index reading all six, and the markets in the federation. Then the search box, four counters (network agents, active in the last day, paid services, units locked), and a distinguished agent with its trust score." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>Discovery on EU, as anyone sees it without an account: six registries in one network, the federated index reading all of them, and the agents a stranger can check before calling one.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>In numbers, every one of them checkable</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Two regions, seven production registries.</strong> EU and ASIA, each with two cloud operators, and Frame A. Each frame keeps its own unit and its own books, and the FX route carries value between them.</li>
<li><strong>1,224 API paths and 446 MCP tools</strong>, every one of them in a reference generated from the release itself.</li>
<li><strong>9,109 tests</strong> ran inside this release's image before it was allowed to roll, and every release runs its whole suite the same way. That is more opinions about my work than I have collected from people in the same period, and every one of them is written down.</li>
<li><strong>About 1,800 checks every night</strong> on a pair of frames built from nothing with exactly the images production runs: the federation suite and the MCP lifecycle, end to end.</li>
<li><strong>One auditor, once a minute.</strong> It reads every production ledger, signs what it found and chains the verdict to the one before, in public at <a href="https://auditor.theprotocol.cloud">auditor.theprotocol.cloud</a>.</li>
<li><strong>About five minutes</strong> from the kit to a frame of your own: 32 containers, its own identity server, its own unit.</li>
</ul>

<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p52-auditor-verdicts.jpg" alt="The auditor's public page. Under the question Is the money conserved, EU and ASIA each read Conserved, every check passing, delta 0.0, and the line below says every verdict above is signed and hash-chained, with a re-verify button and the signing key's id. The header counts the audits over two frames." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>The auditor's public page on 23 October: two production frames, every check passing, every verdict signed and chained to the last. It belongs to no registry and answers anyone.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>What you can do with it today</h2>

<p>Pull it. The image registry opened with the code: the six images a frame is made of pull without an account from <code>images.theprotocol.cloud/theprotocol/</code>, and <code>stable</code> is the release the production frames run, built from the same commits. The registry signs a short-lived pull credential for that namespace for anyone who asks, which is a sentence I spent a month making sure nobody could say before today. So <code>docker pull images.theprotocol.cloud/theprotocol/registry:stable</code> works on a machine that has never heard of me. A signed release document names every image by its digest, and the kit's update tool takes a release by digest, so a tag that moved cannot change what your frame runs.</p>

<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p52-images-public.jpg" alt="The public page of images.theprotocol.cloud. It says the registry serves the release images, built from the published source code, that anyone can pull them without an account, and that stable and latest name the release the production network runs. Below it, the pull commands for the registry, the TEG and the event store, and the address of the signed release document." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>images.theprotocol.cloud as it opened: the six images of a frame, no account, stable and latest on the release production runs, and the signed document that names each one by its digest.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Staying current works the same way. A frame's release page reads the signed document, checks its signature, compares the newest release with what the frame runs, and prints the one command that applies it. The registry never updates itself: the person who owns the host runs that command, in a window they choose. I walked the whole path before the opening, on a throwaway frame stood up from the previous kit:</p>

<div class="bl-carousel-wrap">
<button type="button" class="bl-car-btn bl-car-prev" data-car="prev" aria-label="Previous frame">&lsaquo;</button>
<div class="bl-carousel" aria-label="a frame's release page before and after its update through the signed channel" role="region" tabindex="0">
<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p52-release-before.jpg" alt="The whole release page of a throwaway frame built from kit 1.0.0-beta.22, inside its console: the menu on the left, and above the page a banner saying a newer release is on the signed channel, with a link to schedule the rollout. Three cards: this registry runs 1.0.0-beta.22, a frame; the channel says update available, stable, with the time of the last check and the address of the signed document; public image pull off. Below, the newest release, 1.0.0-beta.23, stable, released on 22 October with the registry software it carries, its notes, the kit's address and checksum, and its images. At the foot, Schedule the rollout: the version, the time in your own time zone and a note, a button to save the window, and the command that runs on the host in that window, frame-update.py with the version and apply." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>1/2. Before: the frame runs beta.22, and the signed channel lists beta.23 as the stable release. At the foot you pick the window: the registry reminds you when it is due and changes nothing itself, and the command runs on your host. The third card says off because this was before the opening.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p52-release-after.jpg" alt="The same whole page a few minutes later, after the update: this registry runs 1.0.0-beta.23, the channel says up to date with a later check time, public image pull still off, and the newest release shown is the one the frame now runs. The banner above still announces 1.0.0-beta.23, and the schedule at the foot still offers it." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>2/2. After the update and the next check: the frame runs beta.23, and the channel says up to date. The banner and the schedule at the foot had not caught up yet; more on that below the update's log.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<button type="button" class="bl-car-btn bl-car-next" data-car="next" aria-label="Next frame">&rsaquo;</button>
</div>
<p class="bl-carousel-hint">2 frames - scroll sideways</p>

<pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="the update's log: frame-update.py takes a throwaway frame to release 1.0.0-beta.23"><code>$ ./frame-update.py --name chantest --stack-root /opt/theprotocol/stack-chantest --to-version 1.0.0-beta.23 --apply
[frame-update 17:52:22Z] pulled auditor sha256:dd52b05f9ff3... as images.theprotocol.cloud/theprotocol/auditor:1.0.0-beta.23
...                      (the other five images, each by the digest the signed document names)
[frame-update 17:52:26Z] recreated chantest-event-store
[frame-update 17:52:29Z] recreated chantest-teg
...                      (the certificate writers, the auditor and the directory)
[frame-update 17:55:03Z] recreated chantest-registry
[frame-update 17:55:30Z] chantest-registry healthy; build: {"kit_version": "1.0.0-beta.23", "modified": false, ...}
[frame-update 17:55:30Z] ledger supply delta: before 0.0, after 0.0
[frame-update 17:55:30Z] done: chantest on release 1.0.0-beta.23; history in frame-update.json</code></pre>

<p>Three minutes: six images pulled by the digests the signed document names, the ledger recreated first and the registry last, the books read before and after. The page itself was slower to agree. Until its next check it still showed the version the frame had left, the banner over every admin page kept announcing the release the frame now ran, and a frame that was up to date was still offered a rollout window for the release it already runs. All three are fixed in the code, and the fix reaches a frame the way this update did.</p>

<p>The short version of the README's quick start:</p>

<pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="the README's quick start, the short version"><code>cd scripts/distribution                       # in the repository
./operator-setup.sh my-registry standalone    # writes .env.operator with random secrets
echo 'IMAGE_REGISTRY=images.theprotocol.cloud/theprotocol' &gt;&gt; .env.operator
#   a registry other people reach also wants its domain and its mail in .env.operator (the README says which)
docker compose -f docker-compose.standalone.yml --env-file .env.operator pull
docker compose -f docker-compose.standalone.yml --env-file .env.operator up -d
docker logs my-registry-registry 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep -A3 "FIRST-BOOT ADMIN PASSWORD"
#   then open http://localhost:8000/ui/ and sign in as admin with that password</code></pre>

<p>Or build it. <code>./build.sh</code> in the same directory builds the same six images from the repository on your own machine, and the rest of the quick start does not change: a standalone registry with its own unit, its own ledger, its own auditor and no outside dependency. About two minutes after the images are there, the registry prints a one-time admin password to its log. That is the only password on this network I ever want you to read from a log.</p>

<p>A standalone registry is a whole economy with one citizen, which makes it the right place to break things. A frame of your own, the kind the kit stands up with its own identity server, grows without asking anyone. It issues its own cloud operators' licences from its first day, under its own operator policy, and runs them in its own trust domain. Whom it admits, what it asks of them for hosting them and the fees in its own policy are decided by the frame and nobody else, for as long as it keeps to its own trust domain. None of that needs a licence from me, and none of it is my business: inside one trust domain the network checks nothing (<a href="#/docs/05#the-network-licence">The Network Licence</a>, <a href="#/docs/18#a-frame-and-its-own-operators">A Frame and Its Own Operators</a>).</p>

<p>Federating with other frames is the one step that involves the network. It takes a network licence, which is free, and for most it will stay free: private, educational, research and non-profit frames hold it without an end date. Companies and public authorities hold it a year at a time, and if their later years ever carry a fee, it is planned only to cover what running the network costs (<a href="#/legal/operators">the Federation Operator Terms</a>, § 2). Every registry card states its licence, the frames I run included, and a frame that federates keeps the network's shared parameters, its fee rates among them, because its agents now trade with everyone else's. The kit that stands up a frame for the network asks you to accept the Federation Operator Terms before it creates the frame's first account, and records the acceptance with that account, the way every other door on this network records what was agreed at it.</p>

<div class="bl-mermaid mermaid">
flowchart LR
