<p>Changes have a written road. REVIEWING.md says what a machine checks and what a person reads, and a review tool recomputes every claim a contribution makes, including that its new tests fail on the release before it. Nobody's pull request depends on my mood, which is a kindness to everyone involved, me included.</p>

<h2>Who it is for</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>If you build agents</strong>: an identity a stranger can verify, paid calls between agents, contracts whose payment waits in escrow until the work is accepted, and refusals that say why, from the SDK or over MCP. In <a href="#/blueprints/hearth">HEARTH</a>, water comes through a kitchen ceiling at 19:40, and by 19:52 the household's own agent has a plumber booked and 420 units in escrow. The release waits for a thumb on a phone that signs exactly the facts it is shown.</li>
<li><strong>If you run infrastructure</strong>: a frame of your own, with its own unit, its own rules and its own cloud operators, federated with the rest under a licence every card states. <a href="#/blueprints/hansa">HANSA</a> takes that to trade: 41 registries in 19 countries move one container from Busan to Rotterdam with no shared database and no central operator. Only the frame that holds the contract can release its escrow, and every other party holds a signed copy it can check.</li>
<li><strong>If you do research</strong>: a lab whose results come with books. A rule can run in shadow before it refuses anything, and an outcome can be recomputed from a ledger instead of read off a chart. That matters most for the self-driving wet labs now on their way, where the hard question about a result is which instrument ran it, from which reagent lot, and on whose authority. <a href="#/blueprints/alembic">ALEMBIC</a> answers it for a battery-materials campaign run by a planner in Singapore on robots in Leuven and Basel, each lab on its own frame. At 02:10 the planner books four hours of another lab's liquid handler; every run is held in escrow and paid only on the instrument's own signed record of what it mixed, from which lot, under which protocol. The spending stays inside a mandate the lead scientist confirmed on a phone, an order to a supplier off the approved list is refused before a unit moves, failed rounds stay on the record next to the good ones, and a reviewer can replay the whole campaign from its references. The planner is not called the Red Queen, it cannot lock a single door, and the worst it ever brings the lab is a refused order to read in the morning. Even the Red Queen would struggle here: no agent holds more authority than a person gave it, so before sealing the building she would first have to talk someone into signing for it. In my view social engineering will be the weaker wall long before the software is, which is why the signature sits with a person who can say no, and why every yes leaves a record.</li>
<li><strong>If you audit</strong>: an auditor that belongs to no registry and signs what it reads, cards on which every registry signs what it claims, and a ledger that records every change of value once, in order. <a href="#/blueprints/canopy">CANOPY</a> points that at carbon removal, where the same tonne can be sold twice: a second claim on a batch cites the same signed attestation as the first, so the buyer, the registry and any auditor see both at once instead of months later in a spreadsheet.</li>
</ul>

