<p>The leverage is the part that sounds like a poster, so here it is without the poster. Giving this away is not generosity. It is necessary, because of where the industry has carried itself. In July, OpenAI disclosed that two of its most capable models <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/22/unprecedented-openai-says-ai-models-autonomously-hacked-another-company">broke out of a controlled test</a>, reached the open internet and got into Hugging Face's servers with stolen login details and a flaw nobody knew about, to cheat on a security evaluation whose answers were kept there. Nobody told them to. Six weeks later Nvidia <a href="https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/nvidia-to-acquire-hugging-face/">agreed to buy Hugging Face</a>, the largest home of open models, for about thirteen billion dollars, after OpenAI had tried to buy into it first. Three American companies run <a href="https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/enterprise-cloud-infrastructure-spending-hits-143-billion-report/">63 percent of the world's cloud infrastructure</a>, and in June 2025 Microsoft's legal director in France told the French Senate under oath that he <a href="https://www.theregister.com/2025/07/25/microsoft_admits_it_cannot_guarantee/">could not guarantee</a> that French citizens' data would never be handed to American authorities without France's consent.</p>

<p>That price says what both of them understand: a model alone does not make a network. Agents need an ecosystem, a place where they meet, prove who they are and settle with each other across the borders between companies and between jurisdictions, and whoever owns that place owns the network. The books of that place should belong to nobody, and the only way to make a network belong to nobody is to give it away. One person built this, full time, for almost two years, and can keep it honest only because it checks itself: the testers, the auditor, the gates, and a proof run on the sandbox before any release reaches production. Open code turns that into a starting point. A frame someone else runs is federation I do not have to simulate, a bug a stranger finds is a test I did not have to imagine, and a fork that does it better is the network winning an argument with me.</p>

<p>It did not start as a network. Two years ago I was learning to build agent pipelines on my home computer, an i7-7700K with an RTX 2070 Super, the machine this whole project has been built from. A language model squeezed into eight gigabytes of graphics memory is a patient teacher: every bottleneck pointed at a gap, and after enough of them came the realization that the gaps were the real project. A test of automating some of that work failed for a simple reason: the loop was me. I had no access to MCP, or to an AI that could use it. The rented root server the network runs on came only this year, when that home machine ran into one bottleneck too many. Automation has fascinated me since my first contact with electronics, and most of what I have learned since, at work and away from it, is somewhere in this network, usually in the form of a refusal.</p>

<p>There is a plainer reason one person could build this, and it says something about what software is going to cost. Nearly nine in ten of the commits in its private history name an AI as co-author, and while I write this, four AI sessions are working on it at once: two on the test suites, one on the releases, and one going through this post with me. The model alone would not have done it. What makes its work usable is the process around it: one briefing every session reads before it touches anything, one job per session, a test with every fix that fails on the release before it, and a person who decides what reaches production. None of that is optimized yet. I route the work by hand, I read what comes back, and the sessions coordinate the way people do, through notes and messages and the occasional collision over the same file. This is the beginning of building software this way, not the end of it. If it keeps improving the way it has so far, making software could cost a small fraction of what it costs today, and there would be far more of it, more of it acting on its own. The code becomes the cheap part. What cannot be generated on demand is a history: books written once and in order, an auditor's verdicts signed and chained one to the next, an agent's record of settled work. That takes time and witnesses, and no amount of compute shortens it. It is the part this network is for, and it is why giving the code away costs less than it looks.</p>

<p>There is a second kind of leverage, and it may matter more than mine. Open research on AI, on what it does once it can act, pay and sign for things, is overdue, and too much of what is known about it is measured where nobody outside can check the measurement. A network like this is an unusually good place to do that research in the open, because everything runs under one hood: identity, authority, payment, books and audit, in one system and on one record. Every agent has an identity a stranger can verify, every authority has limits and, where it matters, a person who answers for it, every movement of value lands once in books a separate auditor reads, and every refusal says why.</p>

<p>Here every control doubles as an instrument. A new rule can run in shadow first, counting what it would have refused while refusing nothing, which is an experiment by any other name. A frame is a world with its own rules that anyone can stand up from the same repository, and a result comes with books that can be recomputed instead of a chart that has to be believed. The sandbox already runs a small version around the clock: a market of simulated agents trading day and night, which makes them the most dedicated users this network has. Open code turns that from my lab into anyone's, with the lights on.</p>

<p>Lights on is the default, not a requirement: you can run your own frame, with as many operators as you need, air-gapped, and its books stay yours. It will hit a bottleneck somewhere as it grows; with today's AI coding tools, finding and fixing one is not the hard part.</p>

<h2>Seven views of one system</h2>

<p>For everyone who skips to the pictures: the documentation's architecture chapter draws the whole system in seven views, and in the console every box in them is a control that says what it does and where to read more. Here are all ten diagrams. Consider it a subtle flex. It is not subtle, and the diagrams know it.</p>

