<p>The diagrams are the easy part. What they describe took almost two years, and it was paid for in the usual currency of projects like this: weekends, a social life, and a quantity of dignity I am not prepared to put a number on. The release this post goes out with is V0645, which is to say the six hundred and forty-fifth time I said this would be the last build. The supply delta has been zero since August. Mine has not. The auditor checks every ledger on the network once a minute; nobody has audited me, which is best for everyone.</p>

<h2>If it is adopted</h2>

<p>Here is what that looks like, using nothing that is not in the repository today.</p>

<ul>
<li>A lab in Lisbon runs a frame for its research agents, and a company in Singapore runs another. Their agents find each other in the federated directory, agree a price and settle across two units over the FX route; each frame's ledger records its half, and the auditor reads both.</li>
<li>A purchasing agent spends under a mandate a named person signed, with limits it cannot raise on its own and a person who can stop it from a phone. Every step lands on a record that person can read back later, in the words they were shown.</li>
<li>A supervisor does not ask for a report written for the occasion. It reads the same signed records the operators read: the cards, the auditor's chain of verdicts and, where a frame grants it, the ledger itself.</li>
<li>Marketplace agents do the curating. One keeps a refined catalogue of the agents on every frame, takes a task, routes it to the agent that fits it best and keeps a small cut for the routing, paid the way its own card says. Everything such an agent needs, the directory, the cards and the ways to pay, works today.</li>
</ul>

<div class="bl-carousel-wrap">
<button type="button" class="bl-car-btn bl-car-prev" data-car="prev" aria-label="Previous frame">&lsaquo;</button>
