<p>So this is not a launch. It is a door. Walk in, read the books, break your own copy, and if you find something, the tests would like a word.</p>

<h2>Patchnotes</h2>

<p>The last post went out with V0606, and the post itself shipped as V0607 and V0608. What follows is <strong>V0609 through V0645</strong>, every version on the sandbox before any production frame. Production took V0613, V0614, V0617, V0618, V0620, V0623, V0625, V0627, V0629, V0631, V0632, V0634, V0635, V0636, V0638, V0644 and V0645 (V0628 rode inside V0629, V0630 inside V0631, V0633 inside V0634, V0637 inside V0638, V0639 to V0643 inside V0644), and the supply delta stayed at zero throughout on every frame the auditor watches. A line that names no place runs wherever the release runs, production included.</p>

<h3>The repository and its documents</h3>

<ul>
<li>The code base is public at github.com/SecureAgentTools/TheProtocol: one commit with a fresh history, the server under AGPL-3.0-or-later and the SDKs under Apache-2.0, with REVIEWING.md, SECURITY.md and the contributor terms.</li>
<li>The README's quick start pulls the release images without an account; <code>./build.sh</code> still builds the same six for anyone who prefers to.</li>
<li>The documentation has one source: the console's doc folder generates the repository's public documents, and the export refuses a copy that differs from it.</li>
<li>The oracle chapters, the operator chapters, the specifications and both papers were read against the code; the API reference and the MCP tool catalog are generated from a release image.</li>
<li>docs.theprotocol.cloud is built from the same source: 68 pages, the console's callouts, dead internal links refused at build, and an llms.txt written from its map.</li>
<li>A new whitepaper, Accountable quantum compute, replaces the analysis of August 2025, and the roadmap is a page of its own (V0611).</li>
<li>The roadmap's Horizon has a second entry, the network deciding its own code, marked as an idea: neither designed nor scheduled (the release that carries this post).</li>
<li>An export refuses a test fixture that carries a server's address (V0618).</li>
<li>A diagram label keeps the type it was measured in, so a label of several lines is no longer cut off (V0629).</li>
<li>The operator pages describe the kit's scripts as they behave, flags and all (V0632).</li>
<li>The console, its documents, the Oracle, the compliance pages and the shipped READMEs describe what a unit does in the words the network's switches stand for: locked units, payouts, units and fees, and issuance. The pages moved to /locks, /units-and-fees and /pocket/payments, and the old addresses redirect (V0642, V0643, V0645).</li>
<li>The API's refusal sentences, the titles and notices it sends back, the registry's mail and the served route texts say the same; codes, keys, paths, operation ids and field names are unchanged (V0643, V0644).</li>
<li>The reference pages document only the routes and tools a frame's switches leave on (V0643).</li>
<li>The published journal names units, TEGs and budgets by their names, and the screenshots of views that have changed since were taken again or removed (V0643, V0645).</li>
<li>A hidden journal post no longer ships its text in the console's bundle, and a test refuses a release that would carry one (V0643).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The security review</h3>

<ul>
<li>Trust between registries is derived from their records and can no longer be set (V0622).</li>
<li>A registry held in quarantine, soft quarantine or on the blacklist takes no part in a slash request, a slash confirmation or a routed fiat purchase (V0621), and a credit arriving from a quarantined trust domain is refused (V0622).</li>
<li>A frame's trust sync names each of its operators by the operator's own identity, never by the frame's domain (V0621).</li>
<li>No operator name may collide with a peer the registry knows, a frame, another operator or the frame's own name, and a few names are reserved (V0622, V0623).</li>
<li>A revocation of a cross-registry delegation is recorded on its issuer first and sent again until the delegate's registry takes it (V0622).</li>
<li>A webhook is delivered to the address that was checked (V0622).</li>
<li>Each slash route answers only for its own caller, and the agent-to-agent payment sweep trusts only its own settlement record (V0622).</li>
<li>A TEG refuses a credential exchange it cannot verify (the production TEGs since 18 October).</li>
<li>An operator writes to its frame's ledger only with an active record there, and a federation vote counts only from an active record (the production ledgers since 18 October).</li>
<li>A leg between two TEGs runs only between current partners: refused otherwise on the sandbox, counted in shadow on production (since 18 October).</li>
<li>A TEG acts on an attestation only when a real verifier accepted its proof (every TEG, since 13 October).</li>
<li>A password recovery link works once (V0636).</li>
<li>A pipeline's webhook address is checked the way every other webhook's is, when it is created, updated, restored from a bundle and sent to (V0644).</li>
<li>Only an administrator's credential marks a pipeline template official (V0644).</li>
<li>Deleting a webhook locks it before its deliveries go, so a delete and a delivery no longer race (V0643).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pocket</h3>

<ul>
<li>A paired phone's key is narrow: removing the phone revokes it, revoking the key disables the phone, no key creates a key with more than itself, and the administrator checks ask the key that was presented (V0618). The key holds the phone's own scope and nothing else (V0622).</li>
<li>The phone's /logo terminal takes the keyboard's focus (V0613).</li>
<li>Pocket, the buy panel on an agent's card and the agent switcher in the header now act as an agent you own on this registry through your session, as the agent picker already did; on a second device, or for an agent made over the API, they had asked for a secret saved in that browser. With several such agents and none chosen, the buy panel asks you to pick one first instead of trying to buy without an agent (V0641).</li>
<li>Pocket's Approvals asks for the owner's rail only where the frame says its phone fabric is on; where it was off, every visit had answered 503 (V0643).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Paying and settling</h3>

<ul>
<li>An answer given again under its Idempotency-Key says so in an Idempotent-Replayed header, which a peer frame's console can read (V0609).</li>
<li>The answers kept under an idempotency key live in the database as well, so a restart cannot forget one (V0610).</li>
<li>A staged payout goes back only on proof that nothing moved, a payment settled by the sweep is announced, and the lost-callback recovery pays only on its own settlement (V0620).</li>
<li>The fee's velocity scaling waits for enough active senders and volume before it moves the fee, and it saturates (V0619).</li>
<li>The FX breaker has a liquidity rule (V0617).</li>
<li>A peer's deactivation releases its TEG partner (V0625).</li>
<li>Every automatic slash update records an event of its own (V0625).</li>
<li>theprotocol-sdk 0.7.6 is on PyPI: an agent that switches payment on now asks for a payment authorization, where 0.7.5 and earlier refused every call with a 422 (20 October).</li>
<li>Accepting a contract asks for a reputation bond on every route, the older one included (V0636).</li>
<li>A dispute filed through the formal route must name an agent the registry knows (V0636).</li>
<li>Engaging an agent whose identifier runs past eighty characters works; the contract kept that identifier in a field eighty characters wide, and the engagement ended in an error (V0638).</li>
<li>An agent spawned without a wallet of its own can work under contracts for the agent whose purse it uses: creating, engaging and accepting act for that agent and ask its checks (accepting, its bond too), while the later steps and reading the contract are left to that agent itself (V0638).</li>
<li>Such an agent now also offers, submits, releases, rejects, cancels and reads those contracts for the agent whose purse it uses; in V0638 those steps refused it as no party to the contract (V0639).</li>
<li>smartSend takes an idempotency key, so a send retried after a timeout cannot move units twice (V0639).</li>
<li>A cross-registry transfer to an agent of the sending registry itself is refused before any route is tried, and nothing moves; on a cloud operator it had gone out through its frame's currency route and come back less two fees (V0639).</li>
<li>A cross-frame settlement can name the asking frame by the address its peers know it by. It named an internal address that every other frame refused, so no settlement ever reached a defendant's frame. The new address waits behind a switch that stays off, because a ruling across frames moves units on both of them, and that path has never run (V0639).</li>
<li>A cross-frame settlement that no peer takes keeps each peer's answer on its record, where it had kept only its count of retries (V0640).</li>
<li>A dispute settlement whose retries are used up is reported once, not every minute, and a refused request no longer counts as a settlement failure in the logs (V0640).</li>
<li>A batch of TEG policies is all or nothing, as its description always said: a policy code named twice is refused before anything is written, and when the TEG refuses one policy, the ones the same call created are switched off again and the call answers with the TEG's reason. Before, a batch whose every policy was refused still answered success, and a later refusal left the earlier ones in force (V0641).</li>
<li>Both FX pool reads name the unit of each figure they give: on EU the administrator's desk had shown ASIA's ACU reserve as ECU, its own reserve as ACU and the spot price's unit the wrong way round. The cross-registry slash and settlement messages name the unit of the frame that writes them, where they had named one frame's unit on every frame, and a refused card payment names the switch that decides it instead of a unit (V0641).</li>
<li>Every label and message that named one frame's unit names the frame's own: the console, the Pocket assistant, the activity feed, four notifications, the dispute texts, the fee estimate and five metrics' help (V0642).</li>
<li>A v2 contract's amount reads as a number on the Contracts strip and in the Contract offered notice; it had printed the server's raw decimal, <code>0E-10</code> on 47 of 50 rows in one screenshot (V0642).</li>
<li>A buy receipt reads its payment again until it settles, instead of keeping the first answer it got (V0643).</li>
<li>The fee rate a route reports is the rate the charge path uses (V0644).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Zero-knowledge attestations</h3>

<ul>
<li>Version 2 of the policy circuits: the limits are public and the figures committed, so a proof binds a committed figure without showing it, and never proves the figure true. The verifier binds the stated public inputs and the registry's policy, version 1 is retired, a replayed proof answers 409, reputation counts once per circuit and day, and the reference string is pinned offline (V0611).</li>
<li>The MCP tester's new lifecycle caught V0611 answering a replayed proof as verified. V0612 answers it with 409 again, and a write that failed answers 503.</li>
<li>The documentation's attestation chapters describe the version 2 circuits, and an agent's profile lists only the attestations that still hold (V0613).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Cards, operators and federation</h3>

<ul>
<li>A registry card promises only what a stranger can use, and names its SPIRE bundle endpoint (V0615, V0617).</li>
<li>EU serves a reach probe, POST /api/v1/public/reach-probe; it is switched off elsewhere (V0615, V0617).</li>
<li>An operator's calls that carry its licence reach its parent over the federation lane (V0614).</li>
<li>One answer of /agent-cards/discover reads one snapshot (V0614).</li>
<li>/metrics is a switch per registry (V0619).</li>
<li>A forked bundle takes the default royalty, and an organization's bundle is shared with its organization (V0620).</li>
<li>A peer's copy of a broadcast carries its words, and a resync registers the peer's ledger (V0620).</li>
<li>A firm on the transparency list always has a feed (V0625).</li>
<li>Two frames that federate register each other's TEG as a partner, when one approves the other's invitation and again when the handshake completes, and a revoke releases the partner (V0630, V0631).</li>
<li>EU's and ASIA's TEGs hold each other as partners, so a leg between the two regions is one the partner rule knows (21 October).</li>
<li>A cloud operator finds its frame's federation lane within fifteen seconds of its boot, where it used to wait five minutes, so its first calls with its licence take that lane (V0632).</li>
<li>An administrator's probe of a peer frame or operator tries its console address when its federation address asks for a client certificate, so the probe no longer reads every peer as unreachable (V0636).</li>
<li>A manual canary fire on a cloud operator says why it cannot run there (an operator fires only when its frame dispatches it) instead of promising a fire within five seconds that never came (V0637).</li>
<li>An administrator's hard delete of an agent takes its card out of the listings with it; the card stayed listed, naming an agent that no longer existed (V0637).</li>
<li>When a frame erases an account, the copies other frames took from it are erased too; a copy had stayed listed with its full content where the receiving frame refuses a card that fails its checks (V0638).</li>
<li>When a frame rejects or approves a settlement another frame asked of it, its answer says whether the asking frame was told; a rejection had said the other frame was notified even when the call back failed (V0638).</li>
<li>A bundle restored or forked into a new agent gives that agent its own linked card, so deleting the agent also takes its card off the listings (V0638).</li>
<li>Lifting a quarantine from a frame that is not quarantined is refused, and nothing changes; the lift had rewritten the frame's admitted policy, which its every later check then read as a drift (V0638).</li>
<li>A broadcast retracted or edited before a peer frame copied it reaches that frame retracted, or with its new words; the copy had arrived after the retraction and stayed live (V0639).</li>
<li>An agent that deletes itself no longer leaves its card listed, here or on the registries that mirror it (V0640).</li>
<li>The MCP tool discoverAgents lists the agents other registries federate here, under the rule the console's Discovery applies, and each page holds at most its limit; it had listed no federated agent at all (V0640).</li>
<li>A route that exists only on a frame now answers a cloud operator with 404 and its reason in the body (mainframe_only, or operator_only the other way round), not 503. A 503 says "try again later", and this route will never be there (V0640).</li>
<li>A card pull carries the agent's name and description into the columns lists are drawn from, so a list shows what the card says (V0644).</li>
<li>Registry-config says which environment a registry runs in, production when unset, and a cloud operator inherits its frame's (V0644).</li>
<li>An agent's enforcement status reads its bond's maturity by the rule the bond route uses, under the policy in force, and the enforcement tutorial teaches it (V0644).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The console</h3>

<ul>
<li>The agent access panel shows its own limits, a picker keeps the latest pick, every dialog closes on Escape, and the Disputes list is whole (V0617).</li>
<li>A website that starts with javascript: is refused (V0617).</li>
<li>The roadmap, the whitepaper and the journal print as the screen shows them (V0613).</li>
<li>The five API tutorials were corrected (V0614).</li>
<li>Discovery's Firms lane keeps its text at 4.5:1 against its background (V0627).</li>
<li>The MCP tester's History draws what each figure is made of and names what is missing, compares a console run only with the previous run on the same frame, and calls a failed step failed, in red (V0627, V0628).</li>
<li>The brand guide page's red lettering is easier to read, and the tutorials hub no longer miscounts the operator tours (V0636).</li>
<li>My Agents, Discovery and the AGORA desk read each listing's last price with one index lookup; before, every page load sorted every trade the market had ever printed, which took 22 seconds on the sandbox under load (V0637).</li>
<li>Each open console page held one database connection for as long as it stayed open, with nothing to end it (75 seconds through one sandbox walk); a page now hands its connection back once it has connected (V0637).</li>
<li>The rest of the brand guide page's small red lettering passes the AA contrast standard, on every section of the page (V0638).</li>
<li>A cross-frame settlement card names each amount in the unit of the frame it belongs to: the slash in the defendant's frame's, the compensation in the asking frame's (V0638).</li>
<li>The console no longer offers to act as an agent that was deleted or is suspended (the server already refused it), and says which when one is chosen (V0640).</li>
<li>Agent Forge offers the templates this registry actually has, and a template it does not have is named in a clear refusal instead of a server error (V0640).</li>
<li>A registry with no showcase services of its own now says so, instead of reading as if its showcase were switched off (V0640).</li>
<li>The buy panel on an agent's card no longer offers Buy for a service agent that the payment step would turn away (for example one without its reputation bond). It shows the payment step's own sentence instead, the one a purchase sent anyway already gets (V0641).</li>
<li>A purchase whose call never reaches the agent no longer shows a paid amount next to "nothing charged", and says plainly that nothing was charged (V0641).</li>
<li>When an agent's address answers with an error page, the buy panel shows the error in words (for example "HTTP 404: Not Found"), never raw data (V0641).</li>
<li>The public showcase only hands a call to another registry when that registry is named for it. A registry with no showcase payer of its own and no named relay lists its paid services without a run and says why (V0641).</li>
<li>The showcase lists a paid service it could not pay for without a run, in the payment step's own words, and its result says SETTLED only when the payment settled, in the unit that was charged, and names the registry that answered when it was not this one (V0641).</li>
<li>Fiat Operations greys out in the sidebar while the euro on-ramp is off, and the header asks for alerts only for an account allowed to read them (V0642).</li>
<li>An administrator's Emission policy page shows the policy in effect for every event type, each peer's rows read at the peer's public address (V0643, V0645).</li>
<li>The flare animations lost their satirical captions; their ids are unchanged, so an equipped flare keeps its animation (V0643).</li>
<li>The blueprints speak of risk where they spoke of cover (AEGIS, DEMETER, HANSA, CADASTRE, HEARTH and the landing row), a new chamber, CAIRN, runs a site where Earth is minutes away or silent, a test refuses a chamber that sells cover, and a capability permit is called a permit (V0644, V0645).</li>
<li>A member's direct link to an administrator's page goes home, with a notice naming the page (V0645).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tests</h3>

<ul>
<li>The API tester acts only on what it made: two-factor setups, liability mandates and developer accounts of its own, a throwaway agent for every administrative action on an agent, never a real peer, and never a credential handover or an operator restart (V0624 to V0627).</li>
<li>A refusal the API tester's contract accepts is a pass only by the route's own sentence, and a failed row carries that sentence (V0625, V0626).</li>
<li>The console suite proves every release on both sandbox frames before production, since V0627 with the sandbox's market simulation running beside it.</li>
<li>The registry's whole suite runs inside every image before it rolls: from more than 7,300 tests at V0606 to 9,109 at V0632, none failed.</li>
<li>The federation suite's last standing red row, a new cloud operator reading its own log through its licence, passes since V0632.</li>
<li>The frame kit's tests keep a clock of their own instead of reading the calendar (V0624).</li>
<li>The MCP tester now carries out more than a hundred more tools for real, on accounts, agents and objects it makes for the run and clears away afterwards: an agent's credentials and signing key rotated, an agent suspended and reinstated, a dispute ruled, an account suspended and restored, a peer registered, approved and retired, a deployment logged and cancelled, organization invitations accepted and withdrawn, a support review granted and revoked, contracts, attestations, locks and account recovery. It used to check only that they turn down a bad request (V0636).</li>
<li>The MCP tester now bids across frames on a task it posts on the other frame, and pays a frame's treasury back the units it gave a test agent (V0636).</li>
<li>A tool that passed only on a correct refusal counts as not measured in the MCP tester's History, and its row says so, so the pass rate counts what was really tested (V0636).</li>
<li>The MCP tester's zero-knowledge attestation rows reach its report again; they had been dropped since V0612 (V0636).</li>
<li>The MCP tester reads the sandbox market's busiest listing for its four market read checks, which had been asking for a listing that no longer exists (V0636).</li>
<li>The console suite walks the operator tours as the frame's administrator, and checks the agent list's Load More whenever there is more to load (24 October).</li>
<li>The console suite hosts a no-change ballot proposal of its own where a frame has none open, so the Senate floor's ballot panels are measured every night (24 October).</li>
<li>The MCP tester's webhook tests deliver to a receiver Sandbox Alpha runs for its own tests, and pass only when it takes the signed delivery; before, every test delivery went to an address that does not exist, so the older of the two test routes was never measured (V0637).</li>
<li>The MCP tester's sweeps of the two sandbox cloud operators now run, on the operator itself, the lifecycles that make their own subjects and clear them away, and name the operator that answered: 54 more rows on each measure what a tool does, beside 17 checked refusals, and the passes that rest on a refusal alone fell to 171 on each, from about 275 in the last count (V0637).</li>
<li>The MCP tester's cross-registry transfer test expects the events of the path its transfer takes; it had read a correct transfer to the frame's own operator as one that did nothing (V0638).</li>
<li>The MCP tester never takes a projection snapshot of a frame; where snapshots are switched on, its probe had taken a real one in every run (V0638).</li>
<li>The MCP tester's cloud-operator sweeps read the operator's own database, never probe the tools that act on the operator itself, and give a test agent's units back to the operator's treasury before deleting it: per operator, 164 tools still pass on a refusal alone, from 171 (V0638).</li>
<li>The MCP tester's cloud-operator sweeps run every lifecycle again except the two whose tools act on the operator itself, clear what each run makes for itself (its probe organizations, API key, support ticket, bundle and TEG policy, and its market listing's treasury agent, with up to 25 of each that earlier runs left), and know the mainframe-only refusal by its words at any status: per operator, 90 tools still pass on a refusal alone, from 164 (V0639).</li>
<li>The MCP tester judges a refusal that names a rail as frame-only or operator-only by its sentence, whichever status the release answers with, so a roll that leaves frames and operators on different releases reads the same (V0639).</li>
<li>The MCP tester measures more on cloud operators: attestation schedules, a vote and an early tally on a proposal of the run's own, and tools that only a frame serves are named as such instead of passing on a refusal (V0640).</li>
<li>The API tester checks the broadcast route with a direct notice to its own throwaway developer; on the sandbox it had sent a broadcast to every developer, which each peer frame copied (V0640).</li>
<li>The MCP tester measures the chat request flow on cloud operators too, with a second account of the run's own, and gives back what its test agents hold before it deletes them (V0641).</li>
<li>On a cloud operator, the tester counts its own market listing and its own TEG policies as the measurements they are, instead of passing those tools on a refusal alone (V0641).</li>
<li>The TEG policy checks now work only on the policies a run makes for itself: an update is read back, and so is a delete. Before, a check could deactivate a policy the run had not made (V0641).</li>
<li>A tool that a cloud operator refuses because the feature is off there, or because only a frame serves it, is reported as not measured, in the operator's own words, instead of passing on that refusal (V0641).</li>
<li>Seven federation tools that only a peer registry can call (three developer tools and four admin tools) are reported as not measured by design, and their refusal is kept as its own check (V0641).</li>
<li>Tools whose success a test run must not drive (finalizing a frame invitation, a dispute settlement, a veto or a raise of a federation proposal) or cannot reach (a relay the test agents do not run) are reported as not measured with the reason on frames and operators alike, and each refusal is checked on an input that reaches the rule (V0641).</li>
<li>The tester buys a free official CI/CD pipeline template for one of its own test agents, so the purchase is measured too; it never buys a priced one (V0641).</li>
<li>On a cloud operator, two features that are off there (the tutorial sandbox and the Grafana query) and the operator's own application history are reported as not measured, with the reason (V0641).</li>
<li>On a cloud operator, the tester's cross-registry transfer check now sends its small test amount to the parent frame's canary receiver in the operator's own unit, instead of to an address nobody holds (V0641).</li>
<li>The federation suite retires every test agent it makes once the test's verdict is in: what the agent holds goes back to the frame's treasury first, then the agent is deleted. Earlier runs had left 4,458 behind on the two sandbox frames; on 25 October a one-time pass gave back their spendable units and deleted 4,321 of them (V0641).</li>
<li>When the federation suite's chat block test misses a control message, its row keeps what the send answered and whether the message reached the host's thread, so a refused relay and a message stored but never passed on no longer read the same (V0641).</li>
<li>The console's vocabulary gate holds English and German to the plan's words with code ceilings at today's counts, and the MCP tester reads every tool listing for the same words and counts the answers (V0643).</li>
<li>A refused write keeps its own failure, and a row that only refuses says why by design (V0643).</li>
<li>The API tester's rules that excused a 500 are gone, 38 of 48, with a guard test and a ceiling of 10 for the rest (V0644).</li>
<li>A renamed MCP tool keeps the response keys gathered under its old name, so a rename cannot hide a gap in a contract (V0644).</li>
<li>Every broadcast the MCP tester composes goes only to the run's own developer; one proof's test broadcasts had reached every console on both sandbox frames (V0645).</li>
<li>The contract lifecycle test's repository is an example.org address, not a GitHub organisation anyone could register (V0645).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The platform</h3>

<ul>
<li>The ledger gate refuses on the six production TEGs since 24 October, after ten days in shadow in which it would have refused nothing; on the sandbox it has refused since 11 October.</li>
<li>The image registry is open: the six release images pull without an account from images.theprotocol.cloud/theprotocol/, stable and latest name the release the production frames run, the release before it keeps its version tag, and the signed release document lists their digests. Pushes stay with the publisher, and EU's registry signs the pull credentials.</li>
<li>Frame kit 1.0.0-beta.23 carries the registry of V0632 and the TEG and ledger the production frames run; a kit frame counts the partner rule in shadow until its trust is proven (kits beta.21 to beta.23).</li>
<li>The kit asks for the Federation Operator Terms before it creates a frame: frame-up.py asks, or takes --accept-terms, and the frame's registry records the acceptance with the first account it issues (V0632, kit beta.23).</li>
<li>The kit's fixes reach the network's own provisioning: the operator CLI's commands do what they print, a bundle's generator writes everything its compose needs to boot on the operator's own host, the standalone ledger takes the registry's and the gateway's writes, frame-update.py --rollback works on a production frame, and a registry whose migration failed refuses to start (in the kits since beta.21, and since 22 October in the templates every new frame and cloud operator is born from).</li>
<li>An administrator's projection snapshot says what became of it: one too large for the ledger's event bus is refused with both sizes named, and one the ledger did not take is answered as not stored; the route had said it took snapshots that never reached the ledger (V0638).</li>
<li>A registry's connections to its TEG, its ledger and its policy engine take their certificates only from a complete set of files. The files are rewritten every five minutes, and a read taken in the middle of a rewrite had been kept until the next one, so those calls could fail for up to five minutes (V0639).</li>
<li>Frame A runs the same release as the other production frames.</li>
<li>Developer tools that answer with a file (the agent builder's two tools and the onboarding bundle) now describe the file: its type, name, size, the files inside and a checksum. They no longer hand back its first characters as text (V0640).</li>
<li>The onboarding bundle checks the agent's secret against its record before it builds; a secret that does not match is refused and no bundle is built, where a mistyped one had built a bundle whose agent could never sign in (V0640).</li>
<li>41 MCP tools carry names in the network's words and still answer to their old names; lock and payout tools are listed only where their switches are on, and arena betting only where it is on (V0643).</li>
<li>The emission policy holds a row for every event type the registry emits. An event type without one is counted and logged as the refusal enforcement would make, in shadow for now (V0643).</li>
<li>The ledger's renamed events are read under either name: by every registry and the console since V0643, by the production ledger stores and the auditor since 26 October, and by the production TEGs since that evening. The writers keep the old names for now.</li>
<li>Every chat fan-out task is held until it is done, the owners' webhook first, and its notices are bounded; a dropped task could vanish mid-flight with its error never read (V0644).</li>
<li>The auditor's public page speaks the same words as the console (26 October).</li>
</ul>

<h2>A quorum of more than one</h2>

<p>Natural language is the new programming language. Anything you can hold in your head as a system, you can now describe until it runs, and every month more people, and more machines, make software that nobody would have called programming a few years ago. Most of it will need what this network is about: an identity a stranger can verify, a limit someone answers for, a way to pay, and books anyone can check.</p>

<p>This network is one person's proof of that, and an attempt to take it further: to democratize how open infrastructure gets built, and to keep it compliant while it opens up. The rules travel with the code, in signed cards, in limits someone answers for and in books an auditor can check, so a frame anyone stands up starts inside them.</p>

<p>When code becomes the cheap part, the scarce part is people: someone to sign the mandate, someone to answer for a frame, enough members to make a vote mean something, and someone to read the books. Everyone is asking what AI will do to their job. Here it did not take mine; it gave me five new ones, and I am still the one who has to say yes. The future I am building has more people in it, not fewer, and it comes with a choice. We can use AI to make more problems, or we can use it to solve the ones we already have, and maybe avoid a few we would otherwise make for ourselves. The second only works if we understand that we are all in this together now.</p>

<p>Building it alone meant learning most of it on the way, which is an excellent way to learn and an exhausting way to work. The hardware I run on has known biological limits: it needs sleep, it runs on coffee, and it has no spare parts. Two kinds of maintenance keep it running: a laugh every few hours, which is why this post has jokes in it, and the gym whenever I can get there, for more than one reason. Besides this network, that is about everything I do. I have done this alone for as long as it could be done alone. A network whose books belong to nobody should not have a single point of failure. Right now it has one, and it needs sleep.</p>

<p>So here is where to start. Every lock on this network was designed by the same person who then tried to pick it, which is exactly as reassuring as it sounds. If you find a way to make the books disagree, <a href="https://github.com/SecureAgentTools/TheProtocol/blob/main/SECURITY.md">SECURITY.md</a> says how to tell me before you tell everyone else, and the advisory names you if you want it. If you know ledgers, identity, interfaces or operations better than I do, and someone out there does, <a href="https://github.com/SecureAgentTools/TheProtocol/blob/main/REVIEWING.md">REVIEWING.md</a> is the road to a corner of your own. The console's newest words wait for every language after English, and each MCP tool that has only been heard saying no needs one test of what it does when it says yes. <a href="https://github.com/SecureAgentTools/TheProtocol/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md">CONTRIBUTING.md</a> has the rest.</p>

<p>When there are enough of us to fill a ballot, I get to be one vote among many, which is the only promotion I have ever wanted. Bring an agent if you like. It will need someone to sign for it.</p>

`
