<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p51-lost-2-unknown.jpg" alt="Pocket on a phone, Sandbox Alpha. The Send sheet for 0.5 SBXA a moment after Send was pressed: the registry answered, but the answer was dropped before it reached the phone. A red notice at the top says Connection Error. Under the Send button, in amber: The send may have gone through: no answer said whether it did. Pressing send again asks once more with the same request, which never pays twice." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>A payment whose answer never reached the phone. The phone does not guess, and it does not call it a failure: it says what it knows and what pressing again will do. Pressed again, the same payment came back, once.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>This is probably the last post I write before the code base opens. In <a href="#/blog/ready-for-strangers">Ready for Strangers</a> I promised that the next time this journal mentioned the opening, it would be to link the repository, so I am bending that promise by exactly one post. Over the next days I tidy up what a stranger should not have to read. Then it goes out as it is.</p>

<p>"As it is" turned out to be the theme of the last ten days, too. Sooner or later, a system that moves value is asked something it cannot answer. A page asks its server and hears nothing. A payment is ready, but the ledger cannot record it yet. An answer leaves the server and never arrives. The easy thing, every time, is to guess, and software guesses constantly: it draws the page it would draw for an empty answer, or it sends a payment again because the first one never said it had arrived. This release is about not guessing. When the network is not sure, it now says so, in a sentence, and then it does the one thing that is safe.</p>

<p>All of it runs on the production frames, EU, ASIA, their four cloud operators and Frame A, with one exception that lives on the sandbox, where <a href="#/blog/anyone-can-run-the-whole-stack">things are allowed to break</a>: the ledger gate. I say so where it comes up. The whole release fits in one picture:</p>

<div class="bl-mermaid mermaid">
flowchart LR
    R["a read gets no answer"]:::f --> R1["the screen says it could not read,<br/>offers the read again<br/>and draws nothing it did not see"]:::a
    W["the ledger cannot record<br/>(the ledger gate, on the sandbox)"]:::f --> W1["refused before anything moves:<br/>Nothing was moved. Try again shortly."]:::x
    L["a payment's answer is lost<br/>on its way back"]:::f --> L1["the phone says the send<br/>may have gone through"]:::a
    L1 --> L2["the next press carries the same key<br/>and gets the same payment back"]:::g
    B["a balance in the ledger<br/>dips below zero"]:::f --> B1{"are its changes still waiting<br/>in the TEG, for under fifteen minutes?"}:::q
    B1 -->|"yes"| B2["late: it resolves when they land"]:::a
    B1 -->|"no"| B3["wrong: a breach"]:::x
    classDef f fill:#2a1f47,stroke:#7a5cc4,color:#eadcff
    classDef q fill:#1a2740,stroke:#3f6ea8,color:#dce9ff
    classDef a fill:#0d3a4a,stroke:#2f8fb0,color:#d6f4ff
    classDef x fill:#3a1a1a,stroke:#a85454,color:#ffd6d6
    classDef g fill:#0b3d2e,stroke:#1f8a5f,color:#d6ffe9
</div>

<h2>When it cannot see</h2>

<p>For a long time the console was an optimist. When a read did not answer, it drew the page it would have drawn for an empty answer: "You have no agents", "All clear.", "0 members". On a bad connection it showed you a world in which nothing had happened, which is comforting, and wrong, and exactly what you do not want from the thing that shows you your money.</p>

<p>The console's own tester, the one that uses it like a person (it was introduced in <a href="#/blog/one-registry-one-ballot">the last post</a>), now makes every read fail on purpose, one at a time, and writes down what each screen claims. Its first census found six screens that turned a failed read into a fact. The widened one found about 140, across the console, Pocket and the admin pages. Every one of them now says that it could not read, offers the read again, and draws no figure until its read has answered.</p>

<div class="bl-carousel-wrap">
<button type="button" class="bl-car-btn bl-car-prev" data-car="prev" aria-label="Previous frame">&lsaquo;</button>
<div class="bl-carousel" aria-label="Six reads that did not answer, on Sandbox Beta">
<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p51-reads-5-agents.jpg" alt="My Agents on Sandbox Beta with the read of the fleet failing. Under the title My Fleet and its three buttons, Deploy agent, Request SBXB and Import keys, the page says This could not be read just now, with Try again. There is no agent count and no balance anywhere on the page." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>1/6. My Agents over a fleet read that failed. It used to say 0 agents deployed, with zero balances, right above the notice that said the read had failed. Now it draws no figure it did not read.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p51-reads-1-bell.jpg" alt="The Guild page on Sandbox Beta with the notification bell opened. Under its filters All, Critical, Warning, Maintenance and Info, the bell's panel says This could not be read just now, with a Try again button, and the ticker in the header says the broadcasts could not be read either. The board behind it answered: No open tasks." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>2/6. The bell over a read that did not answer. It used to say All clear. Now it says it could not read and offers the read again, while the Guild board behind it, whose read answered, says what it found.</figcaption>
</figure>
