</div>
<p class="bl-carousel-hint">6 frames - scroll sideways</p>

<p>Most of them were only misleading. Two could have done real damage. The profile form drew empty fields over a failed read, and Save would have written those empty fields over the real profile. The two-factor panel said "disabled" about an account it had never managed to read, which invites a careful person to switch on something that is already on. A screen that could not see now refuses to act on what it did not see.</p>

<p>The same idea already ran underneath the screens. Since <a href="#/blog/ready-for-strangers">the design review</a>, a permission check that cannot read its data refuses; it never guesses yes. The console has simply caught up with its own server.</p>

<h2>When it cannot record</h2>

<p>Under every frame there are two books. The TEG holds the balances and is the only thing that moves value. The ledger records every change, and the ledger is what the auditor reads and what anyone can check. Between them sits the TEG's outbox, which hands every change to the ledger in order and exactly once.</p>

<p>Until now, an outbox that could not reach its ledger changed nothing at the front. Payments kept going, the TEG kept every balance right, and the record caught up later. For a few seconds that is harmless. For an hour it would mean value moving that nobody can check yet, which is the one thing this network exists to prevent.</p>

<p>So on the sandbox every TEG now runs a ledger gate. While the outbox is healthy, the gate does nothing at all. While the outbox cannot reach the ledger, the gate refuses every request that would start a movement, before anything moves, and says so in one sentence. The registry, the console and Pocket pass that sentence on exactly as it came:</p>

<pre data-lang="the refusal, as the console receives it" tabindex="0"><code>HTTP/1.1 503 Service Unavailable
Retry-After: 30
Content-Type: application/problem+json

{
  "error": {
    "detail": "ledger_unavailable: The ledger cannot record this right now. Nothing was moved. Try again shortly.",
    "type": "service_unavailable",
    "context": {
      "headers": {
        "Retry-After": "30"
      }
    }
  },
  "type": "https://docs.theprotocol.cloud/errors/service_unavailable",
  "title": "Service Unavailable",
  "status": 503,
  "detail": "ledger_unavailable: The ledger cannot record this right now. Nothing was moved. Try again shortly.",
  "instance": "/api/v1/teg/send"
}</code></pre>

<p>What happened, what did not happen, and what to do. The error messages this console used to show managed one of the three, and usually not the useful one. A movement that is already under way is allowed to finish, because stopping a trade between its two halves would be worse than letting it complete; its changes wait in the TEG's journal and reach the ledger, in order, once it is back.</p>

<div class="bl-carousel-wrap">
<button type="button" class="bl-car-btn bl-car-prev" data-car="prev" aria-label="Previous frame">&lsaquo;</button>
<div class="bl-carousel" aria-label="One payment through a ledger outage, on Sandbox Alpha">
<figure>
