<p class="bl-carousel-hint">5 frames - scroll sideways</p>

<p>For these pictures the ledger of Sandbox Alpha was switched off on purpose, on Monday morning, for 36 seconds. The TEG noticed 22.5 seconds in, refused two movements and none after the ledger was back, the changes that had been waiting drained, the books read zero, and the auditor recorded nothing at all. From 18 October the same drill runs by itself every Sunday evening, because a gate that is only tested when I remember to test it has my memory as its weakest part.</p>

<p>A longer run, ninety seconds, showed one more thing. Five payments that Sandbox Beta had already converted toward Sandbox Alpha waited at Sandbox Alpha's FX router until its ledger was back, and reached their receivers five minutes later. Nothing was lost, but a payment should not sit at a router for five minutes, so the frame that converts now asks the far ledger first, and converts nothing while it cannot record.</p>

<p>One small change made restarts honest as well. A frame's TEG used to start under a shell, and a shell that is the first process of its container ignores the polite request to stop, so every restart ended in a kill ten seconds later and a lease that nobody released. I can respect a process that listens to nobody, but not in production. The TEG itself is now the first process of its container, a restart takes about two seconds, and the lease goes with it.</p>

<p>On the production frames the gate comes next. Like every new refusal on this network, its first step there will be to count what it would refuse, without refusing anything.</p>

<h2>When the answer is lost</h2>

<p>The hardest question a payment can ask is whether it went through, when nobody answered. A train goes into a tunnel, a registry restarts, a gateway times out, and somewhere a payment may or may not have happened. The person holding the phone is the one who has to decide whether to press again, and a second press used to be a second payment.</p>

<p>That decision is no longer theirs to get wrong. Every request that moves value carries an idempotency key. The console keeps the key when an answer does not arrive. And since this release, the two routes every payment goes through keep each answer that may have been the payment under its key, for a day, and give it again to anyone who asks with the same key. Late on Monday, on Sandbox Alpha, a payment of 0.5 SBXA was sent from a phone, and its answer was dropped on the way back, the way a tunnel would drop it:</p>

<div class="bl-carousel-wrap">
<button type="button" class="bl-car-btn bl-car-prev" data-car="prev" aria-label="Previous frame">&lsaquo;</button>
<div class="bl-carousel" aria-label="One payment whose answer was lost, on Sandbox Alpha">
<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p51-lost-1-before.jpg" alt="Pocket on a phone, Sandbox Alpha. The Send sheet from the journal's sender agent to its receiver: amount 0.5 SBXA, route LOCAL, they receive 0.5000 SBXA, you pay about 0.5025 SBXA with the 0.5% network fee, paying as the sender on this registry, and the button Send 0.5 SBXA." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>1/4. Before: the same two agents, 0.5 SBXA this time, previewed and ready to send.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p51-lost-2-unknown.jpg" alt="The same sheet after Send was pressed. The registry answered, but the answer was dropped before it reached the phone. A red notice at the top says Connection Error: Unable to connect to the server. Under the Send button, in amber: The send may have gone through: no answer said whether it did. Pressing send again asks once more with the same request, which never pays twice." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>2/4. The first press reached the registry and was paid; its answer never reached the phone. The console's notice says the connection failed, and the sheet says what that means for this payment.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p51-lost-3-again.jpg" alt="The same sheet after Send was pressed a second time: under the buttons, in green, Sent, confirmed." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>3/4. Eleven seconds later the same press, under the same key. The registry answers with the payment it kept from the first press, the same transaction, and nothing moves a second time.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p51-lost-4-after.jpg" alt="Pocket's agent list on the phone, searched for journal51, after both presses: journal51-sender holds 17.99 SBXA and journal51-receiver holds 3.5 SBXA." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>4/4. After: the sender paid 0.5 SBXA and its 0.0025 fee once, and the receiver holds 0.5 more.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<button type="button" class="bl-car-btn bl-car-next" data-car="next" aria-label="Next frame">&rsaquo;</button>
</div>
<p class="bl-carousel-hint">4 frames - scroll sideways</p>

<p>The registry had answered the first press with transaction 4e56d624, and the phone never heard it. The second press carried the same key and got the same transaction back. Two presses, one payment, and nobody had to know why.</p>

<p>The rule behind it is short enough to draw:</p>

<div class="bl-mermaid mermaid">
flowchart TB
    S["a payment arrives with its key"]:::f --> C{"what does the key hold?"}:::q
    C -->|"nothing yet"| RUN["claimed, and kept alive<br/>while the payment runs"]:::a
    C -->|"a send still running"| WAIT["an earlier send under this key<br/>is in progress: ask again shortly"]:::q
    C -->|"an answer"| AGAIN["the same answer, given again:<br/>nothing moves a second time"]:::g
    C -->|"a send that ended<br/>with no answer"| UNK["outcome not known:<br/>never run a second time,<br/>a person reads the ledger"]:::x
    RUN --> A{"what did it answer?"}:::q
    A -->|"proof that nothing moved"| FREE["the key is free again"]:::g
    A -->|"anything else"| KEEP["kept under the key for a day"]:::a
    classDef f fill:#2a1f47,stroke:#7a5cc4,color:#eadcff
    classDef q fill:#1a2740,stroke:#3f6ea8,color:#dce9ff
    classDef a fill:#0d3a4a,stroke:#2f8fb0,color:#d6f4ff
    classDef x fill:#3a1a1a,stroke:#a85454,color:#ffd6d6
    classDef g fill:#0b3d2e,stroke:#1f8a5f,color:#d6ffe9
</div>

<p>A key is freed only by an answer that proves nothing moved: a refusal before any value left. Every other answer is kept and given again. A request that ended with no answer at all, because its server stopped halfway, is answered as exactly that, outcome not known, and is never run a second time. A person reads the ledger and settles it, which is slow, and which is the point.</p>

<p>I would rather say plainly that this rule came late. The AGORA venue's gateway pays traders on other frames through the same send everyone uses, and its key remembered only the payments that succeeded. A payment whose answer was lost left nothing behind, so the same key sent it again, and on the sandbox that had paid fifteen traders twice since September, the last of them during the drill that caught it. A drill, it turns out, is a test that charges admission. The gateway now pays again only when an answer proves the first payment reached nobody, the 985 payouts it had left stranded or marked paid without paying went out after a census of the ledger, each one read back, and its key remembers everything now, not only the good news.</p>

<p>A payment that comes back should come back whole, too. When a gateway turned a route down, its payer used to lose two fees of half a percent on the round trip, so a payment of 0.5 SBXB came back as 0.495012. Now the payer's own frame gives both fees back the moment it learns that the payment came back, and a gateway that can only send a payment back later carries the fees in its refund. The payer receives exactly what it sent.</p>

<h2>Late is not wrong</h2>

<p>The <a href="https://auditor.theprotocol.cloud" target="_blank" rel="noopener">auditor</a> reads every production ledger once a minute, in one statement per frame, and belongs to no registry. One of its checks asks whether anyone's balance in the ledger is below zero. That can happen for an honest reason: the ledger has received a spend while the credit before it is still on its way. Until now the auditor called that a breach. It is not a breach. It is late.</p>

<p>Version 2.5.0 asks the TEG. If an agent's changes are still waiting in the TEG's journal, and the oldest of them is younger than fifteen minutes, the balance is behind and resolves when they land. If nothing is waiting, or something has waited longer than that, it is a breach, as before. Every cycle the auditor also counts the TEG's own negative balances directly, a number that should always be zero, and a count it could not read is reported as not checked, never as zero.</p>

<div class="bl-carousel-wrap">
