    hidden: false,
    excerpt: "Next week the code that runs this network stops being something you have to ask us for, and the question that has been keeping me up is not what happens in the first month but in the first hour: a stranger unpacks an archive on a host that has never heard of us, and either something real happens or this joins the very large pile of repositories that build exactly once, on the author's machine, in the author's presence. Two things had to exist for the answer to be something real. The first is the kit: a release of one commit, with a digest for every file in it, that builds six images on your own host with no account of ours anywhere in it and stands up a sovereign frame with its own currency, its own ledger and its own trust domain. The second is somewhere to break it, because the moment somebody can run this, the next thing they want is to change it, and nobody sane tests a change on the frame their users are on. So a frame from the kit now stands up its own sandbox from its own console: a whole second frame beside it, or a federated pair with a cloud operator each, built through the same doors a real frame walks through, where our full regression sweep of 738 tools runs against the sandbox itself and every write a test makes lands there. Then the loop that turns a place to look at things into a way of working: a baseline on the release, your build on top of it, the same suite again, and a comparison that has to show nothing red that was not red before, packed with the patch into a single archive that refuses to be built if the evidence is not about your change. I walked the whole path as a stranger last night, from the public request form to the comparison, and this post is that run, screenshot by screenshot. Then what it is actually for, what it still does not do, the release we are aiming at next week, and the patch notes since the last post, thirty-three of them.",
    tags: ["Release", "Open source", "Frames", "Testing", "Sandbox", "Handover"],
    content: `
<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p47-1-lead.jpg" alt="The Sandbox panel on the larch frame, finished. Two sandboxes are listed, larchsbx at sandbox.larch.theprotocol.cloud and larchsbxb at sandbox-b.larch.theprotocol.cloud, each with buttons to open its MCP tester, its console and its login. Below them the job reads Provisioning two federated sandboxes, done, 14:53, with eight steps all green: check names lanes and memory, stand up the sandbox, stand up the second sandbox, federate the two sandboxes, a cloud operator on each sandbox, federation suite fixtures, and check the tester can log in, each with a line of detail underneath. A green line reads: ready, open its MCP tester above and run the sweep there. A Tear down button sits at the bottom." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>The thing this post is about: two test frames beside a real one, stood up from the frame's own console in under fifteen minutes, by the same commands that stood up the frame.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Next week the code that runs this network stops being something you have to ask us for. I have written about <a href="/ui#/blog/no-one-owns-the-middle">why nobody should own the middle</a> of a network like this, and about <a href="/ui#/blog/handed-over-once">what a handover has to contain</a> to be worth receiving, so I will not do either again here. This post is about a smaller question that has been keeping me up: what happens in the first hour after somebody downloads it.</p>

<p>Not the first month. The first hour. A stranger unpacks an archive on a host that has never heard of us, and either something real happens or this project joins the very large pile of repositories that build exactly once, on the author's machine, in the author's presence.</p>

<p>Two things had to exist for the answer to be something real. The first is a kit: one commit, packed, that stands a working sovereign frame up on that host. The second is somewhere to break it, because the moment a stranger can run this, the next thing they will want is to change it, and nobody sane tests a change on the frame their users are on.</p>

<p>Both are live, and the way I convinced myself of that was to become the stranger. Yesterday evening I made an account on the flagship that had nothing to do with me, asked for a frame through the public form like anybody else would, approved it from the other side, downloaded the kit through the browser, checked it against its seal, and stood the frame up from nothing but what that page told me. Every screenshot below is from that run.</p>

<h2>What is actually in the box</h2>

<p>The kit is a release of one commit. Not a copy of my working tree, not a tidied subset with the interesting parts filed off: an archive of a commit, with a file beside the code listing the sha256 of every file in it. Twenty-one megabytes, and what it builds is the registry, the unit ledger, the event store, the auditor, the federated index and the certificate writer that this network runs.</p>

<p>On your host, one command builds it. Six images, a few minutes, and no account of ours anywhere in it: no image registry to log in to, no token, nothing to sign up for. It pulls public base images and the packages the release pins, and that is the whole supply chain. The build reads the file list as it goes and stamps what it found into every image: the digest of the tree, whether that tree differs from the release, and which files differ if it does. Each frame then reports that at its own health endpoint.</p>

<p>That last part sounds like bookkeeping and is the reason the rest of this post works. A frame can always answer two questions about itself: which release am I, and has anybody changed me. Later on, those two answers are the whole difference between evidence and an anecdote.</p>

<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p47-kit-steps.jpg" alt="The Your frame page on the Brussels console, seen by the person who asked for a frame. A progress rail reads Requested, Approved, Kit downloaded, Invitation sent, Federated. A checklist titled What your host needs lists Linux with Docker and the Compose plugin version 2.24 or newer, 8 GiB of free memory and 20 GB of free disk, ports 80 and 443 for the console and 8443 to 8445 for the machine lanes, the hostname and its wildcard pointing at this host, and a mail relay. Below, five numbered steps with the exact commands filled in: unpack and build, point your names at the host with two A records, check the host with frame-doctor, start the frame with frame-up, and join the network with frame-federate. Values only the operator knows are highlighted as placeholders. On the right, a card with the kit release 1.0.0-beta.6, 21.5 MB, a Download the kit button, 4 of 5 downloads left, open until Oct 19 2026, and the sha256 of the archive under the heading Check the download." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>The whole handover on one page: what the host needs, the five commands with this frame's own values already in them, and the seal to check the download against. The highlighted values are the ones only the operator knows, and the shell refuses the command if they are left as they are.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Asking for a frame</h2>

<p>There is a form on the Brussels frame for asking for one. Six steps: who you are, what the frame should be called, what its currency is, where it is, how it should join the network, and a review page that shows the registry card the answers will produce. It is a request, not a purchase, and nothing about it is automatic on our side: a person reads it and approves it.</p>

<div class="bl-carousel-wrap">
<button type="button" class="bl-car-btn bl-car-prev" data-car="prev" aria-label="Previous frame">&lsaquo;</button>
<div class="bl-carousel" aria-label="asking for a frame, step by step">
<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/p47-ask-1-frame.jpg" alt="Step two of six of the request form, Your frame. Where it runs offers two cards, Hosted by TheProtocol and On your own infrastructure, the second one selected. Below it, a frame name field reading larch with the note that it is lower case letters, digits and hyphens and cannot change after stand-up, a display name reading Larch Cooperative, a hostname reading larch.theprotocol.cloud, a description, and a colour choice between Neural in cyan and Sovereign in red. On the right, a live preview headed Your registry card, as it takes shape, already showing the name, the description and a genesis issuance of 1,000,000." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>1/3. Step two. The frame name becomes hostnames, containers and a trust domain, which is why the form says so while you are still typing it and refuses to let it change later.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>
