    excerpt: "Nine days ago this network ran on three sovereign frames, the oldest of them going back to the beginning. It now runs on two new ones, Brussels and Singapore, each with its own currency, its own trust domain and two cloud operators, and neither of them existed last week. They were not migrated: they were built from nothing, repeatedly, until nothing had to be added afterwards by a person remembering to, because a network assembled by hand can be described but not repeated and a thing that cannot be repeated cannot be handed to anyone. A frame now receives its federation licence from the frame that admits it, inside the same transaction that approves the join, so ASIA held a licence issued by EU before ASIA existed. Discovery is public, so the question of who is on this network no longer needs an account to ask. The federated index gets its own chapter here: what it is, why an index over sovereign registries has to hold a cryptographic identity, and the bug that only appeared once it had thirteen thousand agents to read instead of fifty five. Cards advertise addresses that answer. And the peers page shows two operators this frame has never spoken to, at two hops, discovered through its peer, which is federation working as a consequence rather than as a diagram. Registry-A, four thousand agents and twenty months old, is being retired rather than migrated. Then the patchnotes, 57 of them.",
