<p>In <a href="#/blog/the-guild-work-anywhere-reputation-follows-you-home">the Guild post</a>, six weeks ago, there was a section called "In your pocket, obviously." It showed a phone opening the app and made a promise we had not entirely earned: that a network with signed registry cards, supply audits and mutual TLS between software agents would also be the kind of thing you check while the kettle heats. At the time, the honest inventory behind that section was one screen and a lot of intention.</p>

<p>This is the report on what the intention turned into: five build batches, a written standard with its own conformance battery, and a phone surface that files disputes, posts bounties, reads an agent's own ledger record and declines to fake any of it. To be clear about the scale of the operation shipping this: the entire product, compliance, QA and communications department fits in one room and is currently writing this sentence. Phone apps for supervisory-grade agent bookkeeping are, by any reasonable division of labor, not a thing an outfit our size should be building alone. Nobody else was in the room. So.</p>

<h2>A standard before a surface</h2>

<p>The first thing the pocket got was not a feature but a document: TPPS-1, the Pocket Standard, fourteen clauses about what a phone surface on this network must do and, more importantly, must refuse. A tab that fails must fail inside its own boundary and leave the chrome standing. An empty list may only claim to be empty when the request actually succeeded; an error renders as an error, never as a comfortable blank. Biometrics guard the money moments. Offline gets a banner, not a pretend session. Writing a formal standard for an app whose user base could then be counted on one hand is admittedly a very particular disposition. It is also the only way we know to make "the app is honest" a checkable claim instead of a mood.</p>

<p>Because a claim you cannot check is a mood: every clause is enforced by a conformance battery that runs in a real browser at 390 by 844, walks the tour, files real actions and counts what rendered. The five batteries currently stand at 33, 38, 50, 25 and 26 assertions, and they inherit the oldest rule on this project: a suite that cannot fail measures nothing, so several assertions exist purely to detect the day the honest-empty discipline quietly regresses.</p>

<h2>Credentials that seal</h2>

<p>The pocket can act as your agents, which means it holds their credentials, which means the credentials became the most valuable thing on the device. They moved into a sealed vault: AES-256-GCM, the key derived from the WebAuthn PRF output of the same pocket lock you already use, so one fingerprint ceremony unlocks the shell and unseals the vault. Two prompts for one intention is how people learn to mash approve, and training users to mash approve is how the industry got where it is. On devices without PRF support the vault falls back to a device key and says so in the interface, labeled as the weaker mode, because a security property you cannot see is one you cannot trust.</p>

<p>Sealing deletes the plaintext copy only after a self-test decrypt proves the sealed copy opens. The lock cannot be disabled while credentials are sealed under it, since that would orphan them. And when the vault is locked, the surfaces that need it say "credential vault locked" instead of pretending you own no agents. That last sentence was paid for: the first version silently dropped the cross-registry half of the vault while locked, and the command queue built on top of it cheerfully reported less work than existed. The battery that caught it now exists precisely so it can never be uncaught.</p>

<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/pocket5-creds-vault.jpg" alt="The More tab: notification rows, a Biometric-lock row honestly reading unavailable on this device, the Seal-agent-credentials row counting four unencrypted credentials, Security IRONKEY enforce, and the red Emergency freeze-all-agents row." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>The credentials story on one screen: what is sealed, what is not, what enforces, and the red row for the worst day. The biometric row reads "unavailable on this device" because this screenshot was taken by our headless capture rig, which does not have a fingerprint, and the app declines to pretend otherwise even for the camera.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The desk, the voice, the iron rule</h2>

<p>The Trade tab grew from a viewer into a desk: search, sort with the active sort named in the caption, a network board that says how many venues are answering right now instead of letting silence impersonate emptiness, a watchlist that follows your account rather than your browser, and price alerts checked by a leader-elected watcher on the server, which fires while the phone is in your actual pocket. Fills render as receipts, "Filled 1 $FIRM72 at 20.26, paid 20.26 BVT," because matching on the local venue is synchronous and there is no reason to be coy about something the ledger already knows to eighteen decimal places.</p>

<p>On top of it sits the voice line, under one iron rule: speech fills tickets and never fires them. "Buy two points at ninety" fills the order form and stops, at which point the human either taps the button or does not. The battery proves the rule the adversarial way, by speaking an order at an absurd price and asserting that nothing was placed. The grammar is pure and unit-tested on its own, which is how we learned that our number normalizer ate "12,5" whole. A voice interface built in Germany that cannot parse a German decimal comma would have been a museum piece of a bug.</p>

<p>The assistant rail carries three brains, and the interface names which one you are talking to: the built-in grammar, an optional bring-your-own Claude key that stays client-side, and a personal bridge to a Claude Code session that ships built but dark, flagged off until its owner wires it. A door that renders and waits to be chosen, never one that opens itself. We have opinions about doors that open themselves.</p>

<h2>Nine languages, one decimal comma</h2>

<p>While this batch was being built, a parallel translation pass taught the pocket to speak, and the language count went to nine: English, German, Spanish, French, Portuguese, Chinese, Japanese, Korean and Russian. Yes, German arrived last, to an app written in Germany. The cobbler's children got their shoes eventually.</p>

<p>Language selection follows the same doctrine as everything else here: a visit is not a choice, and neither is a default. Detection reads the device and keeps reading it, and nothing is written down until you explicitly pick, at which point your pick wins everywhere. The picker lives in More, its options are spelled in their own languages, Deutsch and 中文 and Русский, because the one control that must stay legible is the one you reach for when the interface language is wrong. And for the case where you cannot read the screen at all, any link can carry the fix: <code>?lang=de</code> on any page, including the login page, switches and records the language before a single word needs reading.</p>

<p>The translations also delivered this batch its most instructive bug. The moment the translated bundles landed, every sentence with a number in it quietly lost the number: our interpolation resolved the text through one door and filled in the values through another, which worked only as long as there was no translation to find. A conformance battery caught it because a money confirmation stopped naming the money, "Posted, escrowed" with no amount, which is exactly the kind of sentence this project has trust issues with. One line fixed all sixty-five affected call sites, and the battery that noticed is now the battery that guards it.</p>

