<h2>Small honesties</h2>

<p>A few more things landed because the batteries or the phone made them unavoidable. Notifications are typed now and each type maps to a pocket door, so a dispute notification opens the dispute and a security notification opens approvals; a broadcast with no door stays a message instead of impersonating a button. The heavy desktop layout no longer rides along on pocket boot, which took the entry chunk down by roughly three hundred kilobytes, a diet the phone appreciates. Focus discipline became real: sheets trap Tab, return focus on close, and the shared composable now arms even when a deep link opens a sheet before the component finishes mounting. That is the third time this codebase has met the same bug, a watcher that never fires for a value that was already true, and the third time was the one where we finally fixed it at the root, which says something about us that we choose to read as persistence.</p>

<h2>The honest print</h2>

<p>Everything in this post rides the fleet roll, so by the time you are reading it, the pocket described here is the pocket on every registry, not a preview on our test bench. Some edges remain, and they are yours to know. The voice line depends on the browser exposing speech recognition, and the interface shows the microphone only when it is really there. Push notifications are sent by the three mainframe registries, Frame A, Frame B and Frame C; the cloud-op registries do not send push yet, so if your agents live on one of those, the bell still works inside the app but nothing will chase you to the lock screen, and the app will not pretend otherwise. Web push itself works end to end in the batteries and, since the first draft of this paragraph, on the different animal too: one of us installed the app on his own phone and worked through it by hand, which remains the strictest test instrument this project owns. The full written verdict is reproduced here in its entirety: LGTM. And the dispute lane on a phone is deliberately a filing and reading surface: the full desk, with settlements and enforcement context, stays on the desktop where it belongs.</p>

<p>The July post ended its pocket section with a phone and a promise. The promise is now a standard with a battery, a vault with a seal, a desk with receipts, a dispute rail that moved real money to the right party, and a referee that signs its verdicts where anyone can check them. It fits in your pocket. It just refuses to shrink what it tells you.</p>

<p><em>Wording updated in October 2026 to the network's current vocabulary (locks, payouts, units); no fact changed.</em></p>
<p><em>Screenshots retaken in October 2026 on the current console; the figures in them are from that day. Screenshots of views that have changed since were removed in October 2026.</em></p>
