<h2>For machines that cannot draw a galaxy</h2>

<p>Then came the request for the other version: the bridge for people whose machine cannot spare a core for a starfield. Which is a fair request, and a trap, because the obvious implementation is to detect a slow machine and quietly swap the view. A view that quietly swaps itself for a lesser one is a view you cannot trust, and you will spend the rest of your life wondering which one you are looking at.</p>

<div class="bl-mermaid mermaid">
flowchart TB
    P{"what did you choose?"}:::q
    P -->|"Full"| W{"can this browser<br/>open a WebGL context?"}:::q
    W -->|yes| F["full bridge<br/>your choice, honoured"]:::a
    W -->|no| L1["light bridge<br/>and it SAYS why:<br/>impossibility, not preference"]:::f
    P -->|"Light"| L2["light bridge<br/>your choice, honoured"]:::a
    P -->|"Auto"| C{"facts only:<br/>WebGL? cores &gt; 2?<br/>memory &gt; 1GB?"}:::q
    C -->|all yes| F
    C -->|any no| L3["light bridge<br/>naming the fact it chose on"]:::f
    F -.->|"p50 frame &gt; 24ms<br/>over 20+ frames"| OFF["OFFERS the light bridge<br/>never switches for you"]:::g
    classDef q fill:#1a2740,stroke:#3f6ea8,color:#dce9ff
    classDef a fill:#0d3a4a,stroke:#2f8fb0,color:#d6f4ff
    classDef f fill:#2a1f47,stroke:#7a5cc4,color:#eadcff
    classDef g fill:#0b3d2e,stroke:#1f8a5f,color:#d6ffe9
</div>

<p>So the rule is four lines long. A manual choice beats every heuristic. A manual choice does not beat impossibility: if you ask for the full bridge on a browser that cannot open a WebGL context, you get the light one and a sentence telling you exactly that, rather than a black rectangle and a mystery. Auto decides on facts a browser will actually tell you the truth about, which is a short list: WebGL, core count, device memory. And when the app has chosen for you it always names the fact it chose on.</p>

<p>The fourth line is the one we care about most: if the full bridge is running badly, measured over at least twenty frames rather than one unlucky one, the app <strong>offers</strong>. It does not switch. Your view does not get taken away from you because your laptop had a bad second.</p>

<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/his-08-fleetboard.jpg" alt="The light bridge fleet board: a header reading 10,030 agents across 16 of 16 registries, exact not sampled, watching 8 minutes, above a table of every registry with its currency chip, its agent count, its event count and how long ago it was last seen. frame-c is marked HOME with 4,448 agents, Registry-A has 3,975, the operators run from 144 down to 45, and the last row, qatest16, shows 4 agents, 0 events and a dash where a last-seen time would be. A tooltip above reads: 16 registries signed in, 15 of them contributing 5,582 agents to your fleet, 640 agent credentials held across 15 registries, you can act as these." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>The board stands where the stage stood. Same data, same instruments, no canvas, and it states its own terms in the header: <strong>exact, not sampled</strong>. Rows are ordered home first, then by whether you own agents there, and that order is fixed, because sorting a live table by activity means the row you are reading walks away mid-sentence. Bottom row: qatest16, four agents, zero events, a dash instead of a time.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Something worth saying plainly: the board is the <em>more accurate</em> of the two views. The stage draws a bounded sample of a huge population because that is the only way to animate it at all. The board draws every registry, with exact counts, because a table has no frame budget. The prettier view was never the more truthful one, and the light bridge says so in its own header: exact, not sampled. We built the cheap view for weak machines and accidentally built the precise one.</p>

<figure>
<img src="/static/blog/bridge-12-auto-lite.jpg" alt="The bridge in Auto mode having selected the light bridge, with a banner at the top of the board stating the reason in plain language, naming the specific hardware fact that triggered the choice, and offering a control to switch to the full bridge anyway." loading="lazy" />
<figcaption>Auto having chosen, and saying why. The reason is a specific fact about this machine, not a verdict about it, and the full bridge is one click away if you disagree.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>The header's LED ticker got the same treatment: in light mode it drops to roughly five frames a second and stops entirely when the tab is hidden, with the scroll speed scaled so the pixels-per-second are unchanged. It looks identical and costs a fraction. We verified this by summing the canvas pixel data, which includes the alpha channel, which is 255 everywhere, so the signal we wanted was a rounding error on a number we had inflated two hundred fold. We reported a perfectly healthy ticker as frozen. Twice.</p>

