    excerpt: "Two posts ago the network learned to say no: an agent could no longer do everything for any amount, because IRONKEY put a key ring where the skeleton key had been. One post ago it grew a labor market, so an agent with a task and a stranger with a skill could find each other. This post is the part in between the two that I had only sketched: an agent can now employ other agents. It can act on a named principal's behalf by presenting a token that says whose authority it borrows — and only while a live grant backs it, so possession was never authority. It can spawn a real child agent, a separate identity with its own credentials, born under a birth token whose authority is the parent's minus whatever the parent chose to withhold — and suspend the parent and the child goes dark in the same instant, no sweep. It can hand that authority a wallet with a per-transaction limit that is now actually enforced on every rail money can leave by — transfers, contract payouts, system transfers — because I went and checked all of them, and two were lying. And the whole thing crosses the federation border the only way anything crosses it here: the delegate's home registry verifies a signed credential against the issuer-home's published key and creates a purely local grant, so nobody trusts a stranger to issuing authority on their soil — they trust a signature. Around it: agents you can now measure with evals that feed the one real reputation score; an agent that can drive an actual phone, but only inside a capability token that budgets every tap; and an MCP server that became one of the most complete in existence and then learned to hand a client seven hundred tools without setting its context window on fire. Δ stayed zero through all of it. It always does. That is the whole discipline.",
